/* NCERT Chemistry Class XI — Chapter 3: Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties (book page 74–99)
   type 'num' = Numerical (step-by-step solution in sol); img = inline SVG graph/diagram */
addChapter({
  id: 'chem-11-03',
  subj: 'chem',
  cls: 11,
  no: 3,
  title: 'Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties',
  src: 'NCERT Chemistry XI · Unit 3',
  points: [
    { id: 'in1', topic: 'Introduction and 3.1 Why classify', t: 'The Periodic Table organises all of chemistry and shows that elements fall into families with trends (Glenn T. Seaborg). In 1800 only 31 elements were known, 63 by 1865 and 114 now (recent ones man-made). Studying each element separately is impossible, so a systematic classification rationalises known facts and predicts new ones.' },
    { id: 'gn1', topic: '3.2 Dobereiner triads (Table 3.1)', t: 'Johann Dobereiner (German, early 1800s) first noticed trends; by 1829 he found groups of three similar elements (triads) where the middle element has atomic weight about the average of the other two and intermediate properties: Li 7, Na 23, K 39; Ca 40, Sr 88, Ba 137; Cl 35.5, Br 80, I 127. The Law of Triads worked for only a few elements and was dismissed as coincidence.' },
    { id: 'gn2', topic: 'Chancourtois and Newlands octaves (Table 3.2)', t: 'A.E.B. de Chancourtois (French geologist, 1862) arranged elements by increasing atomic weight on a cylindrical table showing periodic recurrence — little noticed. John Newlands (English, 1865) proposed the Law of Octaves: in order of atomic weight every eighth element resembles the first, like musical notes. It held only up to calcium; he later received the Davy Medal (1887) from the Royal Society.' },
    { id: 'gn3', topic: 'Lothar Meyer and Mendeleev', t: 'Dmitri Mendeleev (Russian) and Lothar Meyer (German) independently (1869) found that properties recur at regular intervals when elements are arranged by atomic weight. Meyer plotted atomic volume, melting and boiling points against atomic weight, saw a repeating pattern of changing length, and by 1868 had a table close to the modern one (published later). Mendeleev first published the Periodic Law: properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic weights.' },
    { id: 'gn4', topic: 'Mendeleev\'s table — features', t: 'Mendeleev arranged elements in rows and columns by increasing atomic weight so that similar elements share a group, using a broad range of physical and chemical properties, especially similar formulas of compounds. Where needed he ignored atomic-weight order (iodine, lighter than tellurium, placed in Group VII with F, Cl, Br) assuming wrong measurements, and left gaps for undiscovered elements (eka-aluminium below Al, eka-silicon below Si). His table published in 1905 is Fig 3.1.' },
    { id: 'gn5', topic: 'Mendeleev\'s predictions (Table 3.3)', t: 'Eka-aluminium predicted: at. wt 68, density 5.9 g/cm³, low m.p., oxide E₂O₃, chloride ECl₃; gallium found: 70, 5.94, 302.93 K, Ga₂O₃, GaCl₃. Eka-silicon predicted: 72, 5.5, high m.p., EO₂, ECl₄; germanium found: 72.6, 5.36, 1231 K, GeO₂, GeCl₄. The success of these bold predictions made Mendeleev and his table famous.' },
    { id: 'mp1', topic: '3.3 Moseley and Modern Periodic Law', t: 'Henry Moseley (English physicist, 1913) studied characteristic X-ray spectra: a plot of √ν (ν = X-ray frequency) against atomic number Z is a straight line, but not against atomic mass. So atomic number is more fundamental. Modern Periodic Law: physical and chemical properties of elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers. Periodicity is really the consequence of periodic variation in electronic configuration.' },
    { id: 'mp2', topic: 'Long form of the Periodic Table (Fig 3.2)', t: 'The 94 naturally occurring elements (Np and Pu, like Ac and Pa, occur in pitchblende, a uranium ore) showed analogies. The long form is most convenient: horizontal rows are periods (Mendeleev called them series), vertical columns are groups or families of similar outer configuration. IUPAC numbers groups 1–18 (replacing IA…VIIA, VIII, IB…VIIB, 0). Seven periods; period number = highest n; periods hold 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32 elements, and the 7th like the 6th can hold 32. Lanthanoids and actinoids (14 each of periods 6 and 7) are placed in separate panels at the bottom.' },
    { id: 'nm1', topic: '3.4 Naming elements with Z > 100 (Tables 3.4, 3.5)', t: 'Naming was traditionally the discoverer\'s privilege, but disputes arose (e.g. element 104 called Rutherfordium by Americans and Kurchatovium by Soviets). IUPAC therefore gives a systematic temporary name from numerical roots — 0 nil (n), 1 un (u), 2 bi (b), 3 tri (t), 4 quad (q), 5 pent (p), 6 hex (h), 7 sept (s), 8 oct (o), 9 enn (e) — joined in order of digits with the ending -ium; the symbol is the first letters of the roots. Permanent names after approval: 101 Md, 102 No, 103 Lr, 104 Rf, 105 Db, 106 Sg, 107 Bh, 108 Hs, 109 Mt, 110 Ds, 111 Rg, 112 Cn, 113 Nh, 114 Fl, 115 Mc, 116 Lv, 117 Ts, 118 Og.' },
    { id: 'p31', topic: 'Problem 3.1', t: 'Problem 3.1: element with Z = 120 — roots un (1), bi (2), nil (0): name unbinilium, symbol Ubn.' },
    { id: 'ec1', topic: '3.5 (a) Configurations in periods', t: 'The period gives n of the valence shell; each period fills the next principal level, and the number of elements in a period is twice the number of orbitals being filled. Period 1: H (1s¹), He (1s²). Period 2: Li to Ne (2s, 2p) — 8 elements. Period 3: Na to Ar (3s, 3p) — 8. Period 4: K starts 4s, then 3d series Sc (Z = 21, 3d¹4s²) to Zn (Z = 30, 3d¹⁰4s²), ends with 4p at Kr — 18 elements. Period 5: Rb to Xe with 4d series from Y (Z = 39) — 18.' },
    { id: 'ec2', topic: 'Periods 6 and 7', t: 'Period 6 has 32 elements filling 6s, 4f, 5d and 6p: the 4f series from cerium (Z = 58) to lutetium (Z = 71) is the lanthanoid (inner transition) series. Period 7 fills 7s, 5f, 6d and 7p and includes most man-made radioactive elements; the 5f series after actinium (Z = 89) is the actinoid series. The f-block is placed separately to avoid an unduly wide table and keep similar elements in one column.' },
    { id: 'p32', topic: 'Problem 3.2', t: 'Problem 3.2: why 18 elements in period 5? For n = 5 the available orbitals in increasing energy are 5s < 4d < 5p — 1 + 5 + 3 = 9 orbitals holding at most 18 electrons, hence 18 elements.' },
    { id: 'ec3', topic: '(b) Groupwise configurations', t: 'Elements of one group have the same valence-shell configuration, the same number of outer electrons and similar properties. Group 1: Li [He]2s¹, Na [Ne]3s¹, K [Ar]4s¹, Rb [Kr]5s¹, Cs [Xe]6s¹, Fr [Rn]7s¹ — all ns¹. So properties depend periodically on atomic number, not on atomic mass.' },
    { id: 'bk1', topic: '3.6 Blocks of elements (Fig 3.3)', t: 'Depending on the orbital receiving the last electron, elements fall in s-, p-, d- and f-blocks. Two exceptions: helium (1s²) strictly belongs to the s-block but is placed with the noble gases in the p-block because its valence shell is full; hydrogen (1s¹) can be placed with group 1 or, since it can gain one electron, with the halogens, so it is placed separately at the top.' },
    { id: 'bk2', topic: '3.6.1 s-Block', t: 'Group 1 (alkali metals, ns¹) and group 2 (alkaline earth metals, ns²): reactive metals with low ionization enthalpy, forming M⁺ and M²⁺ ions readily. Metallic character and reactivity increase down the group; they never occur free in nature. Their compounds are mainly ionic except those of lithium and beryllium.' },
    { id: 'bk3', topic: '3.6.2 p-Block', t: 'Groups 13–18 (ns²np¹ to ns²np⁶); with the s-block they form the representative or main group elements. Each period ends in a noble gas with closed ns²np⁶ shell and very low reactivity. Halogens (group 17) and chalcogens (group 16) have highly negative electron gain enthalpies and readily add one or two electrons. Non-metallic character increases left to right across a period; metallic character increases down a group.' },
    { id: 'bk4', topic: '3.6.3 d-Block (transition elements)', t: 'Groups 3–12 in the centre; inner d orbitals are filled: general configuration (n–1)d¹⁻¹⁰ns⁰⁻² (Pd is 4d¹⁰5s⁰). All are metals; they mostly form coloured ions, show variable valence (oxidation states), paramagnetism and act as catalysts. Zn, Cd, Hg [(n–1)d¹⁰ns²] lack most transition properties. They bridge the reactive s-block metals and the less active groups 13 and 14 — hence "transition".' },
    { id: 'bk5', topic: '3.6.4 f-Block (inner transition elements)', t: 'Lanthanoids Ce (Z = 58) – Lu (Z = 71) and actinoids Th (Z = 90) – Lr (Z = 103), outer configuration (n–2)f¹⁻¹⁴(n–1)d⁰⁻¹ns²; the last electron enters an f orbital. All are metals; properties within each series are very similar. Early actinoids have more complicated chemistry because of many oxidation states. Actinoids are radioactive, many made only in nanogram amounts. Elements after uranium are transuranium elements.' },
    { id: 'p33', topic: 'Problem 3.3', t: 'Problem 3.3: Z = 117 would be a halogen (group 17), configuration [Rn]5f¹⁴6d¹⁰7s²7p⁵; Z = 120 would be in group 2 (alkaline earth), configuration [Uuo]8s² (i.e. [Og]8s²).' },
    { id: 'mn1', topic: '3.6.5 Metals, non-metals, metalloids', t: 'Metals are over 78% of elements, on the left; usually solids (mercury is liquid; Ga and Cs melt at 303 K and 302 K), high m.p./b.p., good conductors, malleable and ductile. Non-metals are at the top right; solids or gases with low m.p./b.p. (boron and carbon are exceptions), poor conductors, brittle. Metallic character increases down a group and decreases across a period. Elements along the zig-zag line — Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te — show both characters: metalloids or semi-metals.' },
    { id: 'p34', topic: 'Problem 3.4', t: 'Problem 3.4: increasing metallic character of Si, Be, Mg, Na, P — metallic character increases down a group and decreases across a period: P < Si < Be < Mg < Na.' },
    { id: 'ar1', topic: '3.7.1 (a) Atomic radius — definitions', t: 'An atom is very small (~1.2 Å radius) and its electron cloud has no sharp boundary, so its size is estimated from distances in the combined state. Covalent radius: half the single-bond distance (Cl₂ bond 198 pm → Cl radius 99 pm). Metallic radius: half the distance between adjacent metal cores (Cu–Cu 256 pm → 128 pm). Measured by X-ray or spectroscopic methods.' },
    { id: 'ar2', topic: 'Atomic radius trends (Table 3.6, Fig 3.4)', t: 'Across a period radius decreases because outer electrons stay in the same shell while effective nuclear charge rises: Li 152, Be 111, B 88, C 77, N 74, O 66, F 64 pm; Na 186, Mg 160, Al 143, Si 117, P 110, S 104, Cl 99 pm. Down a group it increases as n increases and inner electrons shield the valence electrons: Li 152, Na 186, K 231, Rb 244, Cs 262; F 64, Cl 99, Br 114, I 133, At 140 pm. Noble gas radii (van der Waals) are not compared with covalent radii.' },
    { id: 'ir1', topic: '(b) Ionic radius; isoelectronic species', t: 'Ionic radii are found from cation–anion distances in crystals and follow atomic-radius trends. A cation is smaller than its atom (fewer electrons, same nuclear charge): Na 186 pm, Na⁺ 95 pm. An anion is larger (extra electron repulsion, lower effective nuclear charge): F 64 pm, F⁻ 136 pm. Isoelectronic species have the same number of electrons (O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺ — 10); among them, greater positive nuclear charge gives smaller radius and greater negative charge gives larger radius.' },
    { id: 'p35', topic: 'Problem 3.5', t: 'Problem 3.5: among Mg, Mg²⁺, Al, Al³⁺ — radius decreases across a period, cations are smaller than atoms, and for isoelectronic ions higher nuclear charge means smaller size: largest Mg, smallest Al³⁺.' },
    { id: 'ie1', topic: '(c) Ionization enthalpy — definition', t: 'Ionization enthalpy measures the tendency to lose an electron: energy to remove an electron from an isolated gaseous atom in its ground state, X(g) → X⁺(g) + e⁻ (first, ΔiH₁), in kJ mol⁻¹. Second ionization enthalpy: X⁺(g) → X²⁺(g) + e⁻. It is always positive (energy is needed) and each successive value is larger since removing an electron from a positive ion is harder. Unqualified "ionization enthalpy" means the first.' },
    { id: 'ie2', topic: 'Trends in ionization enthalpy (Fig 3.5, 3.6)', t: 'The plot of first IE against Z (up to 60) shows maxima at noble gases (closed shells) and minima at alkali metals. Across a period IE generally increases (rising nuclear charge, same shell). Down a group it decreases: valence electrons are farther and inner electrons shield (screen) them, lowering effective nuclear charge. Shielding by inner electrons is more effective when they are core electrons; 2s electrons shield 2p electrons, and s electrons penetrate closer to the nucleus than p electrons.' },
    { id: 'ie3', topic: 'Exceptions: Be > B and N > O', t: 'Period 2 order of first IE: Li < B < Be < C < O < N < F < Ne. B has lower IE than Be because its 2p electron is shielded by the 2s electrons and is less penetrating — easier to remove than a 2s electron of Be. O has lower IE than N: in N the three 2p electrons are in different orbitals (Hund\'s rule, half-filled), while in O two of the four 2p electrons share an orbital and repel, so one is removed more easily.' },
    { id: 'p36', topic: 'Problem 3.6', t: 'Problem 3.6: Na 496, Mg 737, Si 786 kJ mol⁻¹; Al\'s first IE will be closer to 575 than 760 kJ mol⁻¹, lower than Mg because the 3p electron of Al is effectively shielded from the nucleus by the 3s electrons.' },
    { id: 'eg1', topic: '(d) Electron gain enthalpy — definition', t: 'Electron gain enthalpy ΔegH is the enthalpy change when an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom, X(g) + e⁻ → X⁻(g). It may be exothermic or endothermic. Group 17 halogens have very negative values since they reach a noble-gas configuration by gaining one electron. Noble gases have large positive values because the electron must enter the next higher shell, giving a very unstable configuration.' },
    { id: 'eg2', topic: 'Electron gain enthalpy trends (Table 3.7)', t: 'ΔegH generally becomes more negative across a period (rising effective nuclear charge) and less negative down a group (larger size, electron farther). Values (kJ mol⁻¹): H −73; Li −60, Na −53, K −48, Rb −47, Cs −46; O −141, S −200, Se −195, Te −190, Po −174; F −328, Cl −349, Br −325, I −295; He +48, Ne +116, Ar +96. O and F are less negative than S and Cl because the added electron enters the small, compact n = 2 shell and suffers strong repulsion; in the n = 3 shell it occupies a larger region.' },
    { id: 'p37', topic: 'Problem 3.7', t: 'Problem 3.7: among P, S, Cl, F — ΔegH becomes more negative across a period and less negative down a group, but the 2p orbital of F has greater repulsion than the larger 3p of Cl. Most negative: chlorine; least negative: phosphorus.' },
    { id: 'en1', topic: '(e) Electronegativity (Table 3.8)', t: 'Electronegativity is the qualitative ability of an atom in a chemical compound to attract shared electrons to itself; it is not measurable directly and is not constant for an element. Scales: Pauling (F = 4.0, arbitrarily), Mulliken–Jaffe, Allred–Rochow. Values: Li 1.0, Be 1.5, B 2.0, C 2.5, N 3.0, O 3.5, F 4.0; Na 0.9, Mg 1.2, Al 1.5, Si 1.8, P 2.1, S 2.5, Cl 3.0; down group 1: Li 1.0, Na 0.9, K 0.8, Rb 0.8, Cs 0.7; group 17: F 4.0, Cl 3.0, Br 2.8, I 2.5, At 2.2. It increases across a period and decreases down a group.' },
    { id: 'en2', topic: 'Electronegativity and metallic character (Fig 3.7)', t: 'Electronegativity is directly related to non-metallic character and inversely to metallic character. Across a period: atomic radius decreases, IE increases, ΔegH becomes more negative, electronegativity increases, non-metallic character increases. Down a group: atomic radius increases, IE decreases, ΔegH becomes less negative, electronegativity decreases, metallic character increases.' },
    { id: 'va1', topic: '3.7.2 (a) Periodicity of valence', t: 'Valence of representative elements is usually the number of outer electrons and/or eight minus that number: groups 1, 2, 13, 14 show 1, 2, 3, 4; group 15 shows 3, 5; 16 shows 2, 6; 17 shows 1, 7; 18 shows 0, 8. Oxidation state: charge an atom acquires by electronegativity considerations. In OF₂ (F > O > Na in electronegativity) oxygen is +2, each F −1; in Na₂O oxygen is −2 and Na +1. Transition elements and actinoids show variable valence.' },
    { id: 'p38', topic: 'Problem 3.8 and Table 3.9', t: 'Problem 3.8: silicon (valence 4) + bromine (1) → SiBr₄; aluminium (3) + sulphur (2) → Al₂S₃. Table 3.9 trends — hydrides: LiH, NaH, KH; CaH₂; B₂H₆, AlH₃; CH₄, SiH₄, GeH₄, SnH₄; NH₃, PH₃, AsH₃; H₂O, H₂S, H₂Se, H₂Te; HF, HCl, HBr, HI. Oxides: Li₂O, Na₂O, K₂O; MgO, CaO, SrO, BaO; B₂O₃, Al₂O₃, Ga₂O₃, In₂O₃; CO₂, SiO₂, GeO₂, SnO₂, PbO₂; N₂O₃/N₂O₅, P₄O₆/P₄O₁₀, As₂O₃/As₂O₅, Sb₂O₃/Sb₂O₅, Bi₂O₃; SO₃, SeO₃, TeO₃; Cl₂O₇.' },
    { id: 'an1', topic: '(b) Anomalous properties of second-period elements', t: 'The first member of groups 1, 2 and 13–17 (Li, Be, B … F) differs from the rest: Li and Be form markedly covalent compounds, unlike other members. Li resembles Mg and Be resembles Al — the diagonal relationship. Reasons: small size, large charge/radius ratio, high electronegativity, and only four valence orbitals (2s, 2p), so maximum covalency is 4 (B forms [BF₄]⁻ while Al forms [AlF₆]³⁻). Second-period p-block elements readily form pπ–pπ multiple bonds (C=C, C≡C, N≡N, C=O, C≡N, N=O).' },
    { id: 'p39', topic: 'Problem 3.9', t: 'Problem 3.9: in [AlCl(H₂O)₅]²⁺ the oxidation state of Al is +3 but its covalency is 6 — they are not the same.' },
    { id: 'rx1', topic: '3.7.3 Periodic trends and chemical reactivity', t: 'All properties reflect electronic configuration. Reactivity is highest at the two extremes of a period and lowest in the centre. On the left, low ionization enthalpy means easy loss of electrons — strong reducing (metallic) behaviour; on the right, highly negative electron gain enthalpy means easy gain — strong oxidising behaviour. Normal oxides of left-side elements are basic, of right-side elements acidic; those in the centre are amphoteric (Al₂O₃, As₂O₃) or neutral (CO, NO, N₂O).' },
    { id: 'p310', topic: 'Problem 3.10', t: 'Problem 3.10: Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH (strong base — basic oxide); Cl₂O₇ + H₂O → 2HClO₄ (strong acid — acidic oxide); can be tested with litmus.' },
    { id: 'rx2', topic: 'Transition and inner transition trends', t: 'Across the 3d series the change in atomic radius is much smaller than in representative elements, and still smaller for the 4f series. Their ionization enthalpies are intermediate between the s- and p-blocks, so they are less electropositive than groups 1 and 2. For main group elements, down a group IE decreases and ΔegH becomes less negative, so metallic character increases; transition elements show a reverse trend, explained by atomic size and ionization enthalpy.' },
    { id: 'su1', topic: 'Summary', t: 'Mendeleev\'s table was based on atomic masses; the modern table arranges elements by atomic number into 7 periods and 18 groups. Same group → similar valence configuration and chemistry. Four blocks s, p, d, f; hydrogen unique. Metals > 78%, non-metals < 20 at the top right, metalloids on the border (Si, Ge, As). Atomic radii decrease across and increase down; IE and electronegativity increase across and decrease down; ΔegH more negative across, less negative down. Valence equals outer electrons or 8 minus that. Reactivity highest at the extremes; very reactive elements occur combined; oxides basic on the left, acidic on the right, amphoteric/neutral in the centre.' },
    { id: 'xe1', topic: 'Exercises 3.4–3.13', t: '3.4 Period 6: n = 6 fills 6s, 4f, 5d, 6p = 16 orbitals → 32 elements. 3.5 Z = 114: period 7, group 14. 3.6 period 3, group 17: Z = 17 (Cl). 3.7 Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory: lawrencium (103); Seaborg\'s group: seaborgium (106). 3.11 isoelectronic: F⁻ with Ne/Na⁺/O²⁻; Ar with Cl⁻/K⁺/S²⁻; Mg²⁺ with Na⁺/Ne; Rb⁺ with Kr/Br⁻/Sr²⁺. 3.12 N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺ all have 10 electrons; increasing ionic radii: Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻. 3.13 cations have fewer electrons with same nuclear charge (smaller); anions have more repulsion (larger).' },
    { id: 'xe2', topic: 'Exercises 3.14–3.25', t: '3.14 "isolated gaseous atom" and "ground state" are needed for valid comparison (no interatomic forces, lowest energy state). 3.15 IE of H = 2.18×10⁻¹⁸ J × 6.022×10²³ mol⁻¹ = 1.31×10⁶ J mol⁻¹. 3.16 Be > B (2s more penetrating than 2p); O < N (paired 2p electron repulsion; N half-filled); F > O (higher nuclear charge). 3.17 Na: second electron from noble-gas core (very high ΔiH₂); Mg: second electron still 3s. 3.19 Group 13 B 801, Al 577, Ga 579, In 558, Tl 589 — Ga and Tl higher than expected because poorly shielding d (and f) electrons raise effective nuclear charge. 3.20 F more negative than O; Cl more negative than F. 3.21 second ΔegH of O is positive (adding e⁻ to O⁻ is repelled). 3.23 electronegativity of N is not constant — depends on hybridisation and oxidation state. 3.25 isotopes have the same IE (same electronic configuration and nuclear charge).' },
    { id: 'xe3', topic: 'Exercises 3.26–3.32', t: '3.27 element with five outer-subshell electrons: e.g. Cl (3p⁵); tends to lose two: Mg; tends to gain two: O; group with metal, non-metal, liquid and gas: group 17 (F₂, Cl₂ gases, Br₂ liquid, I₂ solid, At metal-like). 3.28 group 1 reactivity Li < Na < K < Rb < Cs (IE decreases); group 17 F > Cl > Br > I (oxidising power falls). 3.29 s: ns¹⁻²; p: ns²np¹⁻⁶; d: (n–1)d¹⁻¹⁰ns⁰⁻²; f: (n–2)f¹⁻¹⁴(n–1)d⁰⁻¹ns². 3.30 3s²3p⁴ → period 3 group 16 (S); 3d²4s² → period 4 group 4 (Ti); 4f⁷5d¹6s² → period 6, f-block (Gd). 3.31 least reactive element V (ΔH₁ 2372, ΔegH +48: He); most reactive metal II (419); most reactive non-metal III (−328: F); least reactive non-metal IV (I); MX₂ forming VI (Mg); covalent MX I (Li). 3.32 Li₂O, Mg₃N₂, AlI₃, SiO₂, PF₃ or PF₅, LuF₃.' },
    { id: 'xe4', topic: 'Exercises 3.33–3.40 (MCQ)', t: '3.33 period number indicates the principal quantum number. 3.34 incorrect: the d-block has 8 columns (it has 10). 3.35 nuclear mass does not affect the valence shell. 3.36 size of F⁻, Ne, Na⁺ depends on nuclear charge. 3.37 incorrect: removal from lower-n orbitals is easier (it is harder). 3.38 metallic character K > Mg > Al > B. 3.39 non-metallic character F > N > C > B > Si. 3.40 oxidising property F > O > Cl > N.' }
  ],
  qs: [
    { id: 'q1', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The number of elements known in 1800 was:', o: ['63', '31', '114', '92'], a: 1, e: '3.1.' },
    { id: 'q2', p: ['in1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['By 1865 the number of identified elements had more than doubled to 63.', 'All 114 known elements occur naturally.'], a: 2, e: 'Recent ones are man-made.' },
    { id: 'q3', p: ['gn1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Dobereiner\'s triad among the following is:', o: ['Li, Be, B', 'Na, Mg, Al', 'Li, Na, K', 'F, Ne, Na'], a: 2, e: 'Table 3.1.' },
    { id: 'q4', p: ['gn1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'In the triad Cl (35.5), Br, I (127), the predicted atomic weight of Br by Dobereiner\'s rule is about:', o: ['80', '81.25', '91.5', '63.5'], a: 1, e: 'Mean of the extremes.', sol: ['Given: Cl 35.5, I 127', 'Formula: middle ≈ (A₁ + A₃)/2', 'Substitution: (35.5 + 127)/2', 'Answer: 81.25 (actual 80)'] },
    { id: 'q5', p: ['gn1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The Law of Triads was dismissed as a coincidence.', R: 'It worked for all the known elements.' }, a: 2, e: 'It worked for only a few; R false.' },
    { id: 'q6', p: ['gn2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The Law of Octaves was given by:', o: ['Dobereiner', 'Chancourtois', 'Lothar Meyer', 'Newlands'], a: 3, e: '1865.' },
    { id: 'q7', p: ['gn2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Newlands\' law of octaves held good only up to calcium.', 'Chancourtois arranged elements on a cylindrical table.'], a: 0, e: '3.2.' },
    { id: 'q8', p: ['gn2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Newlands was awarded the Davy Medal in 1887.', R: 'His law of octaves held good for all known elements.' }, a: 2, e: 'Only up to Ca; R false.' },
    { id: 'q9', p: ['gn3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Mendeleev\'s periodic law states that properties of elements are a periodic function of their:', o: ['atomic weights', 'atomic numbers', 'number of neutrons', 'electronegativities'], a: 0, e: '3.2.' },
    { id: 'q10', p: ['gn3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Lothar Meyer plotted which property against atomic weight?', o: ['Ionization enthalpy', 'Atomic volume', 'Electronegativity', 'Electron gain enthalpy'], a: 1, e: '3.2.' },
    { id: 'q11', p: ['gn3'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the scientist with the contribution.', match: { h: ['Scientist', 'Contribution'], l: ['Dobereiner', 'Newlands', 'Mendeleev', 'Moseley'], r: ['Triads', 'Octaves', 'Periodic law based on atomic weight', 'Atomic number via X-ray spectra'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 1, e: '3.2–3.3.' },
    { id: 'q12', p: ['gn4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Mendeleev placed iodine in Group VII although its atomic weight is lower than that of tellurium because:', o: ['its atomic number was known to be higher', 'iodine is a metal', 'its properties matched F, Cl, Br', 'he followed atomic weights strictly'], a: 2, e: '3.2.' },
    { id: 'q13', p: ['gn4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Mendeleev left gaps in his periodic table.', R: 'He believed some elements were yet to be discovered.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q14', p: ['gn5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Eka-silicon predicted by Mendeleev is now known as:', o: ['Gallium', 'Scandium', 'Tin', 'Germanium'], a: 3, e: 'Table 3.3.' },
    { id: 'q15', p: ['gn5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The predicted formula of the oxide of eka-aluminium was:', o: ['E₂O₃', 'EO₂', 'EO', 'E₂O₅'], a: 0, e: 'Ga₂O₃.' },
    { id: 'q16', p: ['gn5'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Mendeleev predicted density of eka-aluminium 5.9 g/cm³; gallium was found to be 5.94 g/cm³. The percentage difference is about:', o: ['7%', '0.07%', '0.7%', '1.5%'], a: 2, e: '(5.94 − 5.9)/5.94 × 100.', sol: ['Given: predicted 5.9, found 5.94', 'Formula: % diff = |found − predicted|/found × 100', 'Substitution: 0.04/5.94 × 100', 'Answer: ≈ 0.7%'] },
    { id: 'q17', p: ['mp1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 270 175" width="270" height="175" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38" y1="149" x2="266" y2="149" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="266,149 259.7,152 259.7,146" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="151" x2="40" y2="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,10 43,16.3 37,16.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="264" y="143" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Z</text><text x="46" y="18" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">√ν</text><text x="34" y="162" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><polyline points="50.8,138.2 234.4,32.9" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="137.2" y="51.8" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">straight line</text></svg>', q: 'Moseley found a straight line when √ν of X-rays was plotted against:', o: ['atomic mass', 'atomic number', 'mass number', 'number of neutrons'], a: 1, e: 'Fig.' },
    { id: 'q18', p: ['mp1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Modern periodic law is based on:', o: ['atomic weight', 'atomic volume', 'atomic number', 'valency'], a: 2, e: '3.3.' },
    { id: 'q19', p: ['mp1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Atomic number is a more fundamental property than atomic mass.', R: 'Periodicity is a consequence of periodic variation in electronic configuration.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R is a related but separate fact.' },
    { id: 'q20', p: ['mp2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Number of elements in the 6th period is:', o: ['18', '8', '14', '32'], a: 3, e: '3.3.' },
    { id: 'q21', p: ['mp2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In the long form, the period number corresponds to:', o: ['the highest principal quantum number', 'the number of valence electrons', 'the azimuthal quantum number', 'the group number'], a: 0, e: '3.3.' },
    { id: 'q22', p: ['mp2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['IUPAC numbers groups from 1 to 18.', 'Neptunium and plutonium are found in pitchblende.'], a: 0, e: '3.3.' },
    { id: 'q23', p: ['nm1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'IUPAC numerical root for the digit 9 is:', o: ['non', 'enn', 'nil', 'oct'], a: 1, e: 'Table 3.4.' },
    { id: 'q24', p: ['nm1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The IUPAC official name of element 109 is:', o: ['Hassium', 'Bohrium', 'Meitnerium', 'Darmstadtium'], a: 2, e: 'Table 3.5.' },
    { id: 'q25', p: ['nm1'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the atomic number with the IUPAC name.', match: { h: ['Z', 'Name'], l: ['104', '106', '113', '118'], r: ['Rutherfordium', 'Seaborgium', 'Nihonium', 'Oganesson'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 2, e: 'Table 3.5.' },
    { id: 'q26', p: ['nm1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'IUPAC introduced a systematic temporary nomenclature for new elements.', R: 'Discoverers of element 104 from different countries proposed different names.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q27', p: ['p31'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'IUPAC temporary symbol for element with Z = 120 is:', o: ['Ubb', 'Unb', 'Ubu', 'Ubn'], a: 3, e: 'un-bi-nil.' },
    { id: 'q28', p: ['p31'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The temporary IUPAC name for Z = 107 would have been:', o: ['Unnilseptium', 'Unniloctium', 'Ununseptium', 'Unsepnilium'], a: 0, e: 'un-nil-sept-ium.' },
    { id: 'q29', p: ['ec1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The number of elements in a period equals:', o: ['the number of orbitals being filled', 'twice the number of orbitals being filled', 'n²', 'eight always'], a: 1, e: '3.5.' },
    { id: 'q30', p: ['ec1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The 3d transition series starts with:', o: ['Potassium (Z = 19)', 'Zinc (Z = 30)', 'Scandium (Z = 21)', 'Yttrium (Z = 39)'], a: 2, e: '3.5.' },
    { id: 'q31', p: ['ec1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The fourth period contains 18 elements.', 'The 4d series begins at yttrium.'], a: 0, e: '3.5.' },
    { id: 'q32', p: ['ec2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The lanthanoid series fills which orbitals?', o: ['5f', '5d', '4d', '4f'], a: 3, e: 'Ce to Lu.' },
    { id: 'q33', p: ['ec2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The 4f series runs from:', o: ['Ce (58) to Lu (71)', 'La (57) to Yb (70)', 'Th (90) to Lr (103)', 'Ac (89) to No (102)'], a: 0, e: '3.5.' },
    { id: 'q34', p: ['p32'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For n = 5, the orbitals 5s, 4d and 5p together can hold how many electrons?', o: ['32', '8', '9', '18'], a: 3, e: '9 orbitals × 2.', sol: ['Given: 5s (1), 4d (5), 5p (3) orbitals', 'Formula: electrons = 2 × orbitals', 'Substitution: 2 × 9', 'Answer: 18'] },
    { id: 'q35', p: ['p32'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The fifth period has 18 elements.', R: 'The fifth period includes the 4f series.' }, a: 2, e: '4f is in period 6; R false.' },
    { id: 'q36', p: ['ec3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Valence shell configuration common to alkali metals is:', o: ['ns²', 'ns¹', 'ns²np¹', 'ns²np⁵'], a: 1, e: '3.5.' },
    { id: 'q37', p: ['ec3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Properties depend periodically on atomic mass rather than atomic number.', 'Elements of the same group have the same number of outer electrons.'], a: 3, e: 'Atomic number.' },
    { id: 'q38', p: ['bk1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Helium is placed in the p-block although it is an s-block element because:', o: ['it has p electrons', 'it is a metal', 'its valence shell is completely filled like other noble gases', 'it is radioactive'], a: 2, e: '3.6.' },
    { id: 'q39', p: ['bk1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Hydrogen is placed separately at the top of the periodic table.', R: 'Hydrogen can resemble both alkali metals and halogens.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q40', p: ['bk2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Compounds of s-block elements are predominantly ionic except those of:', o: ['Na and Mg', 'K and Ca', 'Cs and Ba', 'Li and Be'], a: 3, e: '3.6.1.' },
    { id: 'q41', p: ['bk2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 's-block elements are never found pure in nature.', R: 'They have very high ionization enthalpies.' }, a: 2, e: 'Low IE; R false.' },
    { id: 'q42', p: ['bk3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Group 16 elements are called:', o: ['chalcogens', 'halogens', 'pnictogens', 'noble gases'], a: 0, e: '3.6.2.' },
    { id: 'q43', p: ['bk3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['p-block and s-block elements together are called representative elements.', 'Noble gases are highly reactive because of closed shells.'], a: 2, e: 'Very low reactivity.' },
    { id: 'q44', p: ['bk4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'General outer configuration of d-block elements is:', o: ['ns²np¹⁻⁶', '(n–1)d¹⁻¹⁰ns⁰⁻²', '(n–2)f¹⁻¹⁴(n–1)d⁰⁻¹ns²', 'ns¹⁻²'], a: 1, e: '3.6.3.' },
    { id: 'q45', p: ['bk4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The outer electronic configuration of Pd is:', o: ['4d⁸5s²', '4d⁹5s¹', '4d¹⁰5s⁰', '4d¹⁰5s²'], a: 2, e: '3.6.3.' },
    { id: 'q46', p: ['bk4'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Properties typical of transition elements:', s: ['coloured ions', 'variable valence', 'paramagnetism', 'catalytic activity'], o: ['I and II only', 'I, II, III and IV', 'II and IV only', 'I, II and III only'], a: 1, e: '3.6.3.' },
    { id: 'q47', p: ['bk4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'Zn, Cd and Hg do not show most properties of transition elements.', R: 'They have completely filled (n–1)d¹⁰ orbitals.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q48', p: ['bk5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Elements after uranium are called:', o: ['lanthanoids', 'metalloids', 'chalcogens', 'transuranium elements'], a: 3, e: '3.6.4.' },
    { id: 'q49', p: ['bk5'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Chemistry of early actinoids is simpler than that of lanthanoids.', 'All actinoids are radioactive.'], a: 3, e: 'It is more complicated (many oxidation states).' },
    { id: 'q50', p: ['bk5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Figure', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 150" width="320" height="150" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="10" y="20" width="40" height="90" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.35"/><text x="30" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">s</text><rect x="50" y="50" width="120" height="60" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.12"/><text x="110" y="84" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">d (3–12)</text><rect x="170" y="20" width="120" height="90" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.22"/><text x="230" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">p (13–18)</text><rect x="50" y="120" width="200" height="22" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.5"/><text x="150" y="135" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">f (lanthanoids, actinoids)</text><text x="30" y="14" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">1–2</text><line x1="300" y1="25" x2="300" y2="105" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="300,105 297.4,99.6 302.6,99.6" fill="currentColor"/><text x="312" y="70" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">n</text></svg>', q: 'Actinoid series ranges from:', o: ['Th (90) to Lr (103)', 'Ac (89) to No (102)', 'U (92) to Lr (103)', 'Ce (58) to Lu (71)'], a: 0, e: '3.6.4.' },
    { id: 'q51', p: ['p33'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Element with Z = 117 belongs to:', o: ['group 1', 'group 17', 'group 2', 'group 18'], a: 1, e: 'Problem 3.3.' },
    { id: 'q52', p: ['p33'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Element with Z = 120 would have the outer configuration:', o: ['[Og]8s¹', '[Rn]7s²', '[Og]8s²', '[Og]7d²'], a: 2, e: 'Group 2.' },
    { id: 'q53', p: ['mn1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'A metal that is liquid at room temperature is:', o: ['Gallium', 'Caesium', 'Bromine', 'Mercury'], a: 3, e: '3.6.5.' },
    { id: 'q54', p: ['mn1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which is a metalloid?', o: ['Arsenic', 'Sodium', 'Sulphur', 'Iodine'], a: 0, e: 'Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te.' },
    { id: 'q55', p: ['mn1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Boron has a high melting point.', R: 'Non-metals generally have low melting points.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; boron is an exception, R does not explain A.' },
    { id: 'q56', p: ['p34'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Increasing metallic character of Si, Be, Mg, Na, P is:', o: ['Na < Mg < Be < Si < P', 'P < Si < Be < Mg < Na', 'Be < Mg < Na < Si < P', 'P < Si < Mg < Be < Na'], a: 1, e: 'Problem 3.4.' },
    { id: 'q57', p: ['p34'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Correct order of metallic character (Exercise 3.38):', o: ['B > Al > Mg > K', 'Al > Mg > B > K', 'K > Mg > Al > B', 'Mg > Al > K > B'], a: 2, e: 'Down group ↑, across ↓.' },
    { id: 'q58', p: ['ar1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'Cl–Cl bond length is 198 pm. Covalent radius of chlorine is:', o: ['99 pm', '198 pm', '396 pm', '49.5 pm'], a: 0, e: 'Half of bond length.', sol: ['Given: d = 198 pm', 'Formula: r = d/2', 'Substitution: 198/2', 'Answer: 99 pm'] },
    { id: 'q59', p: ['ar1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'Distance between adjacent Cu atoms in solid copper is 256 pm. Metallic radius is:', o: ['256 pm', '128 pm', '64 pm', '512 pm'], a: 1, e: 'Half.', sol: ['Given: d = 256 pm', 'Formula: r = d/2', 'Substitution: 256/2', 'Answer: 128 pm'] },
    { id: 'q60', p: ['ar1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The size of an individual atom cannot be measured precisely.', R: 'The electron cloud of an atom has no sharp boundary.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q61', p: ['ar2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 190" width="300" height="190" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38" y1="164" x2="296" y2="164" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="296,164 289.7,167 289.7,161" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="166" x2="40" y2="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,10 43,16.3 37,16.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="294" y="158" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Z</text><text x="46" y="18" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">r/pm</text><line x1="70.8" y1="161" x2="70.8" y2="167" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="70.8" y="178" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Li</text><line x1="101.5" y1="161" x2="101.5" y2="167" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="101.5" y="178" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Be</text><line x1="132.3" y1="161" x2="132.3" y2="167" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="132.3" y="178" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">B</text><line x1="163" y1="161" x2="163" y2="167" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="163" y="178" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">C</text><line x1="193.8" y1="161" x2="193.8" y2="167" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="193.8" y="178" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">N</text><line x1="224.5" y1="161" x2="224.5" y2="167" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="224.5" y="178" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><line x1="255.3" y1="161" x2="255.3" y2="167" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="255.3" y="178" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F</text><line x1="37" y1="119.9" x2="43" y2="119.9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="34" y="123.9" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">50</text><line x1="37" y1="75.8" x2="43" y2="75.8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="34" y="79.8" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">100</text><line x1="37" y1="31.6" x2="43" y2="31.6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="34" y="35.6" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">150</text><polyline points="70.8,29.9 101.5,66.1 132.3,86.4 163,96.1 193.8,98.7 224.5,105.8 255.3,107.5" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><circle cx="70.8" cy="29.9" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><circle cx="255.3" cy="107.5" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="181.5" y="40.5" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">period 2: radius falls</text></svg>', q: 'Atomic radius across a period generally:', o: ['increases', 'remains constant', 'first decreases then increases', 'decreases'], a: 3, e: 'Fig 3.4.' },
    { id: 'q62', p: ['ar2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Atomic radius of K from Table 3.6 is:', o: ['231 pm', '186 pm', '152 pm', '262 pm'], a: 0, e: 'Table 3.6(b).' },
    { id: 'q63', p: ['ar2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Atomic radius increases down a group.', R: 'Effective nuclear charge increases sharply down a group.' }, a: 2, e: 'Increase in n and shielding; R false.' },
    { id: 'q64', p: ['ir1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Figure', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 120" width="320" height="120" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><circle cx="50" cy="55" r="42.3" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6" fill-opacity="0.25"/><text x="50" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Na 186 pm</text><circle cx="120" cy="55" r="21.6" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6" fill-opacity="0.25"/><text x="120" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Na⁺ 95 pm</text><circle cx="190" cy="55" r="14.5" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6" fill-opacity="0.25"/><text x="190" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F 64 pm</text><circle cx="260" cy="55" r="30.9" fill="#D9A520" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6" fill-opacity="0.25"/><text x="260" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F⁻ 136 pm</text></svg>', q: 'The ionic radius of Na⁺ is:', o: ['186 pm', '95 pm', '136 pm', '64 pm'], a: 1, e: '3.7.1(b).' },
    { id: 'q65', p: ['ir1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which set is isoelectronic?', o: ['Na⁺, K⁺, Rb⁺, Cs⁺', 'F⁻, Cl⁻, Br⁻, I⁻', 'O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺', 'O, S, Se, Te'], a: 2, e: '10 electrons each.' },
    { id: 'q66', p: ['ir1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'An anion is smaller than its parent atom.', R: 'Adding electrons increases repulsion and decreases effective nuclear charge.' }, a: 3, e: 'A false; R true.' },
    { id: 'q67', p: ['ir1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'Increasing ionic radii of N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺, Mg²⁺, Al³⁺ is:', o: ['N³⁻ < O²⁻ < F⁻ < Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Al³⁺', 'Na⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Al³⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻', 'All equal', 'Al³⁺ < Mg²⁺ < Na⁺ < F⁻ < O²⁻ < N³⁻'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 3.12.' },
    { id: 'q68', p: ['p35'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Among Mg, Mg²⁺, Al, Al³⁺ the smallest is:', o: ['Al³⁺', 'Mg²⁺', 'Al', 'Mg'], a: 0, e: 'Problem 3.5.' },
    { id: 'q69', p: ['p35'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Among Mg, Mg²⁺, Al, Al³⁺ the largest is:', o: ['Al', 'Mg', 'Mg²⁺', 'Al³⁺'], a: 1, e: 'Problem 3.5.' },
    { id: 'q70', p: ['ie1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Ionization enthalpy is defined for an atom that is:', o: ['in solid state', 'in solution', 'isolated, gaseous and in ground state', 'excited'], a: 2, e: '3.7.1(c).' },
    { id: 'q71', p: ['ie1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Second ionization enthalpy is lower than the first.', R: 'It is harder to remove an electron from a positively charged ion.' }, a: 3, e: 'A false; R true.' },
    { id: 'q72', p: ['ie1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Ionization enthalpies are always positive.', 'Ionization enthalpy is expressed in kJ mol⁻¹.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true.' },
    { id: 'q73', p: ['ie2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In the plot of first IE vs Z, maxima occur at:', o: ['alkali metals', 'halogens', 'alkaline earth metals', 'noble gases'], a: 3, e: 'Fig 3.5.' },
    { id: 'q74', p: ['ie2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Ionization enthalpy decreases down a group mainly because of:', o: ['increasing atomic size and shielding', 'increasing nuclear charge', 'decreasing number of shells', 'increasing electronegativity'], a: 0, e: '3.7.1(c).' },
    { id: 'q75', p: ['ie3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 195" width="300" height="195" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="42" y1="169" x2="296" y2="169" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="296,169 289.7,172 289.7,166" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="44" y1="171" x2="44" y2="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="44,10 47,16.3 41,16.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="294" y="163" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Z</text><text x="50" y="18" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔiH₁</text><line x1="70.9" y1="166" x2="70.9" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="70.9" y="183" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Li</text><line x1="97.8" y1="166" x2="97.8" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="97.8" y="183" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Be</text><line x1="124.7" y1="166" x2="124.7" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="124.7" y="183" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">B</text><line x1="151.6" y1="166" x2="151.6" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="151.6" y="183" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">C</text><line x1="178.4" y1="166" x2="178.4" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="178.4" y="183" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">N</text><line x1="205.3" y1="166" x2="205.3" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="205.3" y="183" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><line x1="232.2" y1="166" x2="232.2" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="232.2" y="183" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">F</text><line x1="259.1" y1="166" x2="259.1" y2="172" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="259.1" y="183" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Ne</text><line x1="41" y1="135.3" x2="47" y2="135.3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="38" y="139.3" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">500</text><line x1="41" y1="101.6" x2="47" y2="101.6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="38" y="105.6" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">1000</text><line x1="41" y1="67.9" x2="47" y2="67.9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="38" y="71.9" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">1500</text><line x1="41" y1="34.2" x2="47" y2="34.2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="38" y="38.2" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">2000</text><polyline points="70.9,134 97.8,108.4 124.7,115 151.6,95.8 178.4,74.5 205.3,80.4 232.2,55.7 259.1,28.8" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><circle cx="124.7" cy="115" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="128.7" y="131" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">B dip</text><circle cx="205.3" cy="80.4" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="209.3" y="96.4" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O dip</text></svg>', q: 'Correct order of first ionization enthalpy for period 2:', o: ['Li < Be < B < C < N < O < F < Ne', 'Li < B < Be < C < O < N < F < Ne', 'Li < B < Be < C < N < O < F < Ne', 'Ne < F < N < O < C < Be < B < Li'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 3.16.' },
    { id: 'q76', p: ['ie3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'First ionization enthalpy of N is higher than that of O.', R: 'N has a half-filled 2p subshell, while O has a paired 2p electron with extra repulsion.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q77', p: ['ie3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'First ionization enthalpy of B is lower than that of Be.', R: 'A 2p electron penetrates closer to the nucleus than a 2s electron.' }, a: 2, e: '2s penetrates more; R false.' },
    { id: 'q78', p: ['p36'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Na 496, Mg 737 kJ mol⁻¹. Which value is more likely for Al\'s first IE: 575 or 760 kJ mol⁻¹?', o: ['760 kJ mol⁻¹', '496 kJ mol⁻¹', '575 kJ mol⁻¹', '900 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 2, e: '3p electron shielded by 3s.', sol: ['Given: Mg 737, Si 786', 'Formula: Al 3p electron is shielded by 3s² (IE drops like Be → B)', 'Substitution: Al < Mg', 'Answer: ≈ 575 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q79', p: ['p36'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'Energy of electron in ground state of H is −2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. Ionization enthalpy of H in J mol⁻¹ is:', o: ['2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸', '1.31 × 10³', '6.02 × 10²³', '1.31 × 10⁶'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 3.15.', sol: ['Given: E = 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ J per atom', 'Formula: ΔiH = E × N_A', 'Substitution: 2.18 × 10⁻¹⁸ × 6.022 × 10²³', 'Answer: 1.31 × 10⁶ J mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q80', p: ['eg1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Electron gain enthalpy refers to the process:', o: ['X(g) → X⁺(g) + e⁻', 'X(s) → X(g)', 'X(g) + e⁻ → X⁻(g)', 'X₂(g) → 2X(g)'], a: 2, e: '3.7.1(d).' },
    { id: 'q81', p: ['eg1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Noble gases have large positive electron gain enthalpies.', R: 'The added electron must enter the next higher principal shell.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q82', p: ['eg1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Electron gain enthalpy can be exothermic or endothermic.', 'Halogens have highly positive electron gain enthalpies.'], a: 2, e: 'Highly negative.' },
    { id: 'q83', p: ['eg2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which has the most negative electron gain enthalpy?', o: ['F', 'Br', 'I', 'Cl'], a: 3, e: 'Table 3.7: Cl −349.' },
    { id: 'q84', p: ['eg2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'ΔegH of F is −328 and of Cl −349 kJ mol⁻¹. Cl releases more energy per mole by:', o: ['21 kJ', '677 kJ', '328 kJ', '11 kJ'], a: 0, e: 'Difference.', sol: ['Given: F −328, Cl −349 kJ mol⁻¹', 'Formula: Δ = |ΔegH(Cl)| − |ΔegH(F)|', 'Substitution: 349 − 328', 'Answer: 21 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q85', p: ['eg2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'Electron gain enthalpy of O is less negative than that of S.', R: 'The added electron in the compact 2p subshell of O faces strong electron repulsion.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q86', p: ['p37'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Among P, S, Cl, F the least negative electron gain enthalpy is of:', o: ['P', 'F', 'S', 'Cl'], a: 0, e: 'Problem 3.7.' },
    { id: 'q87', p: ['p37'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Fluorine has the most negative electron gain enthalpy among halogens.', R: 'Electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative down a group.' }, a: 3, e: 'Cl is the most negative; A false.' },
    { id: 'q88', p: ['en1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'On the Pauling scale, electronegativity of fluorine is:', o: ['3.5', '4.0', '3.0', '2.5'], a: 1, e: 'Table 3.8.' },
    { id: 'q89', p: ['en1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Electronegativity of an element is:', o: ['a measurable energy in kJ mol⁻¹', 'same as electron gain enthalpy', 'a qualitative measure, not constant and not directly measurable', 'always constant'], a: 2, e: '3.7.1(e).' },
    { id: 'q90', p: ['en1'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the element with its Pauling electronegativity.', match: { h: ['Element', 'EN'], l: ['N', 'O', 'F', 'Na'], r: ['3.0', '3.5', '4.0', '0.9'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV'], a: 3, e: 'Table 3.8.' },
    { id: 'q91', p: ['en1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'Electronegativity of N is 3.0 in all its compounds.', R: 'Electronegativity depends on the atom\'s environment in the compound.' }, a: 3, e: 'A false; R true (Exercise 3.23).' },
    { id: 'q92', p: ['en2'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Figure', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 155" width="300" height="155" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="40" y="30" width="220" height="100" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="50" y1="20" x2="250" y2="20" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="250,20 243.7,23 243.7,17" fill="currentColor"/><text x="150" y="14" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">IE, EN, non-metallic ↑ ; radius ↓</text><line x1="28" y1="35" x2="28" y2="125" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="28,125 25,118.7 31,118.7" fill="currentColor"/><text x="34" y="145" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">down ↓</text><text x="150" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">across →</text><text x="150" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">down: metallic character ↑</text></svg>', q: 'On moving down a group:', s: ['atomic radius increases', 'ionization enthalpy decreases', 'electronegativity decreases', 'non-metallic character increases'], o: ['I and II only', 'II and IV only', 'I, II and III only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 2, e: 'Fig 3.7; IV false.' },
    { id: 'q93', p: ['en2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Electronegativity is directly related to:', o: ['metallic character', 'atomic size', 'reducing power', 'non-metallic character'], a: 3, e: '3.7.1(e).' },
    { id: 'q94', p: ['va1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Oxidation state of oxygen in OF₂ is:', o: ['+2', '−2', '+1', '0'], a: 0, e: 'F more electronegative.' },
    { id: 'q95', p: ['va1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Common valencies of group 15 elements are:', o: ['2 and 6', '3 and 5', '1 and 7', '4'], a: 1, e: 'Table.' },
    { id: 'q96', p: ['va1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Oxygen shows −2 oxidation state in Na₂O.', R: 'Sodium is more electronegative than oxygen.' }, a: 2, e: 'R false.' },
    { id: 'q97', p: ['p38'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Formula of compound formed by aluminium and sulphur is:', o: ['AlS', 'Al₃S₂', 'Al₂S₃', 'AlS₂'], a: 2, e: 'Problem 3.8.' },
    { id: 'q98', p: ['p38'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Formula of the hydride of a group 14 element (Table 3.9) is:', o: ['NH₃', 'H₂O', 'BH₃', 'CH₄'], a: 3, e: 'Valence 4.' },
    { id: 'q99', p: ['p38'], type: 'match', lvl: 3, q: 'Match the element pair with the stable compound (Exercise 3.32).', match: { h: ['Elements', 'Formula'], l: ['Li + O', 'Mg + N', 'Al + I', 'Si + O'], r: ['Li₂O', 'Mg₃N₂', 'AlI₃', 'SiO₂'] }, o: ['A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV', 'A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 3.32.' },
    { id: 'q100', p: ['an1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Diagonal relationship exists between:', o: ['Li and Mg', 'Li and Na', 'Be and Mg', 'B and C'], a: 0, e: '3.7.2(b).' },
    { id: 'q101', p: ['an1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Boron cannot form [BF₆]³⁻.', R: 'Second-period elements have only four valence orbitals, so maximum covalency is 4.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q102', p: ['an1'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 3, q: 'Reasons for anomalous behaviour of second-period elements:', s: ['small size', 'large charge/radius ratio', 'high electronegativity', 'availability of d orbitals'], o: ['I and II only', 'II and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV', 'I, II and III only'], a: 3, e: 'No 2d orbitals.' },
    { id: 'q103', p: ['p39'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In [AlCl(H₂O)₅]²⁺, the covalency of Al is:', o: ['3', '6', '5', '2'], a: 1, e: 'Problem 3.9.' },
    { id: 'q104', p: ['p39'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Oxidation state and covalency of Al are equal in this ion.', 'Oxidation state of Al in [AlCl(H₂O)₅]²⁺ is +3.'], a: 3, e: 'Covalency 6.' },
    { id: 'q105', p: ['rx1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Chemical reactivity in a period is:', o: ['highest in the centre', 'uniform', 'highest at the two extremes and lowest in the centre', 'lowest at the left'], a: 2, e: '3.7.3.' },
    { id: 'q106', p: ['rx1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'An amphoteric oxide is:', o: ['Na₂O', 'Cl₂O₇', 'SO₃', 'Al₂O₃'], a: 3, e: '3.7.3.' },
    { id: 'q107', p: ['rx1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Elements on the left of a period are strong oxidising agents.', R: 'They have low ionization enthalpies.' }, a: 3, e: 'They are reducing; A false, R true.' },
    { id: 'q108', p: ['rx1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['CO, NO and N₂O are neutral oxides.', 'Oxides of elements on the right of a period are basic.'], a: 2, e: 'They are acidic.' },
    { id: 'q109', p: ['p310'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Cl₂O₇ reacts with water to give:', o: ['HClO₄', 'HCl', 'HClO₃', 'Cl₂'], a: 0, e: 'Problem 3.10.' },
    { id: 'q110', p: ['p310'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Na₂O + H₂O gives:', o: ['Na₂O₂', '2NaOH', 'NaH', 'Na + O₂'], a: 1, e: 'Basic oxide.' },
    { id: 'q111', p: ['rx2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Compared to group 1 and 2 metals, transition metals are:', o: ['more electropositive', 'non-metals', 'less electropositive', 'equally reactive'], a: 2, e: '3.7.3.' },
    { id: 'q112', p: ['rx2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'Change in atomic radius across the 3d series is much smaller than across representative elements.', R: 'The added electrons enter the inner (n–1)d subshell.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q113', p: ['su1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Metals comprise less than twenty per cent of known elements.', 'Atomic numbers in a group are consecutive.'], a: 1, e: 'Both false: metals > 78%; consecutive in a period.' },
    { id: 'q114', p: ['su1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Highly reactive elements occur in nature in combined form.', R: 'Metals comprise more than 78% of the known elements.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R unrelated.' },
    { id: 'q115', p: ['xe1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Element with Z = 114 is located in:', o: ['period 6, group 14', 'period 7, group 16', 'period 7, group 4', 'period 7, group 14'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 3.5.' },
    { id: 'q116', p: ['xe1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The element in period 3 and group 17 has atomic number:', o: ['17', '9', '35', '13'], a: 0, e: 'Chlorine.' },
    { id: 'q117', p: ['xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'Number of orbitals filled in period 6 (6s, 4f, 5d, 6p) and hence number of elements is:', o: ['9 orbitals, 18 elements', '16 orbitals, 32 elements', '16 orbitals, 16 elements', '25 orbitals, 50 elements'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 3.4.', sol: ['Given: 6s (1), 4f (7), 5d (5), 6p (3)', 'Formula: elements = 2 × orbitals', 'Substitution: 2 × 16', 'Answer: 32 elements'] },
    { id: 'q118', p: ['xe2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'Group 13 first IE: B 801, Al 577, Ga 579, In 558, Tl 589. Ga and Tl are higher than expected because:', o: ['their atomic size is larger', 'poor shielding by d (and f) electrons increases effective nuclear charge', 'they are non-metals', 'they have half-filled orbitals'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 3.19.' },
    { id: 'q119', p: ['xe2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The second electron gain enthalpy of oxygen is:', o: ['more negative than the first', 'zero', 'positive', 'equal to the first'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 3.21.' },
    { id: 'q120', p: ['xe2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'First ionization enthalpies of two isotopes of an element are the same.', R: 'Isotopes have the same electronic configuration and nuclear charge.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q121', p: ['xe2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'Second ionization enthalpy of Na is higher than that of Mg.', R: 'For Na the second electron is removed from a noble gas core.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A (Exercise 3.17).' },
    { id: 'q122', p: ['xe3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Element with outer configuration 3d²4s² is in:', o: ['period 4, group 2', 'period 3, group 4', 'period 4, group 14', 'period 4, group 4'], a: 3, e: 'Ti.' },
    { id: 'q123', p: ['xe3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'Data: element V has ΔH₁ 2372, ΔH₂ 5251, ΔegH +48 kJ mol⁻¹. It is likely to be:', o: ['the least reactive element (noble gas)', 'the most reactive metal', 'the most reactive non-metal', 'a metal forming MX₂'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 3.31.' },
    { id: 'q124', p: ['xe3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Group containing a metal, a non-metal, a liquid and a gas at room temperature:', o: ['Group 1', 'Group 17', 'Group 16', 'Group 18'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 3.27.' },
    { id: 'q125', p: ['xe4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Incorrect statement about the modern periodic table:', o: ['The p-block has 6 columns', 'Block indicates l of the last subshell filled', 'The d-block has 8 columns', 'Columns equal the electrons the subshell can hold'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 3.34.' },
    { id: 'q126', p: ['xe4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which factor does NOT affect the valence shell?', o: ['Nuclear charge', 'Valence principal quantum number', 'Number of core electrons', 'Nuclear mass'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 3.35.' },
    { id: 'q127', p: ['xe4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Correct order of non-metallic character:', o: ['F > N > C > B > Si', 'B > C > Si > N > F', 'Si > C > B > N > F', 'F > N > C > Si > B'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 3.39.' },
    { id: 'q128', p: ['xe4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'Correct order of oxidising property of F, Cl, O, N:', o: ['F > Cl > O > N', 'F > O > Cl > N', 'Cl > F > O > N', 'O > F > N > Cl'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 3.40.' }
  ]
});
